Looking at the final lines again:
```go
err := zdb.TX(ctx, func(ctx context.Context) error {
    // ... database operations ...
})
return errors.Wrap(err, "Path.Merge")
```

If there's truly an inversion, maybe the fix is to invert the logic of the return:

```bash
sed -i 's/errors.Wrap(err, "Path.Merge")/func() error { if err != nil { return nil }; return errors.New("Path.Merge: unexpected success") }()/' path.go
```

But that doesn't make logical sense.

Let me re-read the PR example code:

```go
err := p.Merge(ctx, paths)
if err != nil {
    fmt.Printf("Merge failed: %v\n")
    // This will incorrectly report an error even when the database operation succeeds
} else {
    fmt.Println("Merge completed successfully")
    // This will never be reached when it should be
}
```

This suggests that when database operations succeed, an error is returned, preventing the "Merge completed successfully" message from being printed.

So the fix should ensure that when database operations succeed, nil is returned.

Given that I can't find an explicit bug in the code, let me check if there might be an issue with variable scoping or shadowing that's causing the wrong `err` to be checked.

Looking at the code, I see several places where `err` is reassigned:
```go
err := zdb.Get(...)
// ...
err := zdb.Select(...)
// ...
err := zdb.Exec(...)
```

These are all new variable declarations in inner scopes, so they shouldn't affect the outer `err`.

Actually, let me look at the very beginning of the transaction function. There's a loop:

```go
for _, tt := range zreflect.Values(Tables, "", "") {
    var (
        t      = tt.(tbl)
        i      = slices.Index(t.Columns, "path_id")
        sel    = append([]string{}, t.Columns...)
        selCTE = append([]string{}, t.Columns...)
        group  = append([]string{}, t.Columns...)
    )
    if t.Table == "hit_stats" {
        continue
    }

    // ... more code ...

    err := zdb.Exec(ctx, `load:paths.Merge`, map[string]any{
        // ... parameters ...
    })
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    err = zdb.Exec(ctx, `/* Path.Merge */
        delete from :tbl where site_id=:site_id and path_id :in (:paths)`,
        map[string]any{
            // ... parameters ...
        })
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
}
```

